int/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a Linear System With Variables Other Than x, y, z"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087554 :question/id #uuid "b798cfbb-89ac-5d31-adb5-5c43bd483226" :question/math-academy-id "e-5374" :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](images/15/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293 :topic/math-academy-id 803 :topic/title "Graphing Curves Defined Parametrically"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048811 :topic/math-academy-id 842 :topic/title "Equations of Circles Centered at the Origin"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291 :topic/math-academy-id 112 :topic/title "Properties of the Unit Circle in the First Quadrant"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a576c9f2-a206-5267-ab56-5a854c5e0bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 16\\sin θ, y = 8\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084861} {:answer/id #uuid "a60d4df1-1c39-553a-be3a-063bbbac083b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 