0b3d70618e7fdce2237bdcbfd017c23c01b80f2979.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-4,5)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-4,5).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146d3b28-8e29-5eb2-9b1c-13a2a90ec385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399498} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d5787b-b5bd-5b27-a033-31a126fb604a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399499} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd95806-d79f-5932-8502-3d798b511be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399500} {:answer/id #uuid "c7286f56-415b-5975-9965-cac2f25bbe61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399501} {:answer/id #uuid "4df757e2-103b-56a4-a77e-0738b69bd499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4d5787b-b5bd-5b27-a033-31a126fb604a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399499} :answer-field/id #uuid "05f37ecc-6d85-5118-a9aa-9f9f01af2578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399497}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "656ebe17-b886-53ff-b069-a7253f396457" :question/math-academy-id "q-106049" :question/problem "![](images/21/2135f70f6703878d5f82d3524c890af08e194d7fdfbe92e97a5305d142f73779.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(2,1)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(2,1).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f37153f-6b80-5708-b6a4-edebf7c3a823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399505} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b71958-0b7a-58d5-aa67-e69e50d38e5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399506} {:answer/id #uuid "e501da01-42b8-58ee-9609-f73020f69f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399507} {:answer/id #uuid "08a3ceb4-1e5e-5d85-b566-0b5a554e82e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399508} {:answer/id #uuid "08282cf0-c65d-5a51-98dd-3a937c9e9dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08a3ceb4-1e5e-5d85-b566-0b5a554e82e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399508} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b61b62a-5e5d-5935-a620-97cbe92e0582" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399504}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c45d1161-cc6e-56e5-89da-543c3e758d02" :question/math-academy-id "q-106051" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-3,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087616 :question/id #uuid "8bff424d-8f2e-528b-acf0-12c33958a132" :question/math-academy-id "e-6817" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049488 :topic/math-academy-id 207 :topic/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94805d06-84ce-573c-95d0-ef9240045ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 3)^{2} + (y - 5)^{2} = 81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084855} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b96777-b8f0-5318-a701-8952fe3121b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 3)^{2} + (y - 5)^{2} = 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084856} {:answer/id #uuid "bda79002-269a-55da-805c-50c6a85976b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 3)^{2} + (y + 5)^{2} = 81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084857} {:answer/id #uuid "466785f2-f5ab-544a-9f83-653479ce6953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 3)^{2} + (y + 5)^{2} = 18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084858} {:answer/id #uuid "67d8c3a4-81c0-5500-9673-e670303fba2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 3)^{2} + (y - 5)^{2} = 18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084859}] :answer-field/id #uuid "31b498e5-1477-59a7-b414-140c1ef6e234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084854}] :question/id #uuid "e0808c46-a050-5e50-a062-e87353dcdd48" :question/math-academy-id "q-15799" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$x = 9\\cos θ - 3, y = 9\\sin θ + 5, 0 \\le θ < 2π$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4e3ff7-0f56-5670-968d-942fa26f266b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333116} {:answer/id #uuid "57b7ce5d-ec6b-5250-87a2-d5986283a00b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333790} {:answer/id #uuid "743a1c09-f9a8-5517-b74f-0ae55c1bb3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334038} {:answer/id #uuid "85baee00-733f-595d-aeb8-248060ee0eaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334179} {:answer/id #uuid "a77f4060-9ba3-5f34-8f8a-5b05fd704d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a77f4060-9ba3-5f34-8f8a-5b05fd704d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334457} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd786231-9ee2-54a9-ac19-aa1cf56c5c9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335616}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e86c521-0742-549b-8e0a-9a85599dcb9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6879" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =9(1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f59f06d-b9a6-5438-a966-d3b5565735a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239446} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4e0cba-3407-5463-bc8e-cecffd5ecc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239559} {:answer/id #uuid "b0bebb9d-7faf-5639-8545-a824ed4d0302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+2)}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239592} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a220fb-a7b7-5add-a148-72170d71b738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239764} {:answer/id #uuid "ff94d839-5539-5e72-b58a-5e6b2133fac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad4e0cba-3407-5463-bc8e-cecffd5ecc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239559} :answer-field/id #uuid "43343ab1-d9ca-5b6d-a323-22a64344f342" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240446}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2bc3b27-8c03-5cde-b344-2e0c55032f69" :question/math-academy-id "q-106299" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(y+2) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(x+1)}^{2}+4{(y+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation of the circle is ${(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1179ddc8-9d2b-5f37-858d-a4af5d525038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238199} {:answer/id #uuid "5c77ee41-eb12-5d5d-aee0-f73a63e3d59a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238864} {:answer/id #uuid "868b4972-5724-52d5-879b-b7ce9a27b554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239226} {:answer/id #uuid "e91810a1-61c1-5eba-91a0-ed9b1bf5ef6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240107} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3e3abf-1c31-5d04-ae31-72a0f1e5bc40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c77ee41-eb12-5d5d-aee0-f73a63e3d59a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238864} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f97f707-7649-51de-a352-c860d9645f7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240416}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "85bc2cb1-6e4a-5b13-b0c1-a6335dc66a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-106300" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa416b1-d5ce-5503-b94a-3e38eba55fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241276} {:answer/id #uuid "56eae8d6-2606-519e-88c0-2a4f95d5a1ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241966} {:answer/id #uuid "6b02e3f2-9739-5323-a096-d7c64f08da33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242140} {:answer/id #uuid "94adb393-4580-5bd9-8341-d1156639d308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242486} {:answer/id #uuid "c970979d-e0eb-5a39-af93-dcdfdeddae68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56eae8d6-2606-519e-88c0-2a4f95d5a1ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241966} :answer-field/id #uuid "c999e49c-1d23-5427-99dc-13feea41bd21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243793}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f054c8b-83ad-51a3-ac06-f9b576f40779" :question/math-academy-id "q-106696" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{25}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ba1d767-a5eb-570e-83d3-118be64e32a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273447} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0ba066-dba6-518e-800f-bc01062cfe89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274319} {:answer/id #uuid "df8ba9fa-4cfe-5b42-8b8c-710472617b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274361} {:answer/id #uuid "e18d5d4d-6070-502f-bc7c-e09775e0034f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274378} {:answer/id #uuid "ed64d68f-de54-52ff-b004-27654116e004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ba1d767-a5eb-570e-83d3-118be64e32a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273447} :answer-field/id #uuid "f98238ed-0fcb-5faf-a467-c183d1b64324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275077}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2463b954-08db-52f2-9a44-620e52671ff5" :question/math-academy-id "q-15793" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4(1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ac3088-b004-5942-a759-305e66bd829a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275671} {:answer/id #uuid "6de4ee6e-8cf1-5e32-9477-62eec85dc8e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276459} {:answer/id #uuid "84c9d846-ee11-5df6-b4f3-ad301f646c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276667} {:answer/id #uuid "a2132d3d-04bc-511e-8fa8-498a58febd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276936} {:answer/id #uuid "e7431f1c-0e17-521f-8eb7-8db114d02b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14ac3088-b004-5942-a759-305e66bd829a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275671} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb6ca943-4b04-5351-9712-89b82d0fb09b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278169}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aceee4b9-7657-5a9b-99d8-00f2a88fd80d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15845" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(x-1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(y-2) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the Cartesian equation of the circle is $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a911054-c5bb-5029-8afa-8d953ec079d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a911054-c5bb-5029-8afa-8d953ec079d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/