 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=36.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b4ea447-f2d0-526c-9ddf-83da34800f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b4ea447-f2d0-526c-9ddf-83da34800f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300744} :answer-field/id #uuid "800b3d0b-20d9-50e8-a5e7-06f6f3966edc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301630} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf960976-4b33-5d50-bb00-f55f8bea67a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf960976-4b33-5d50-bb00-f55f8bea67a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300997} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9c94416-2a0c-5eec-9d8c-84444f96eb1f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301966} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e37b2f7b-15e2-5184-bb38-7c4d0c13df3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e37b2f7b-15e2-5184-bb38-7c4d0c13df3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301094} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec807056-656f-52ca-a946-3ed532a26982" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301979}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7267f9b7-cdd2-51d4-b411-b6d5bc974cb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-321986" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+3}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=25.$"} 